title the number stops being a guess
prompt ran both scripts, rebuilt and logged in. seat the derived-spine key so row numbers stop colliding.
think The design already existed and I read it before writing a line -- Move 1 of the 20260825.205011 design, which the tree had recommended and left at Keaton's door through six firings. Seating a law the tree has already argued means implementing the argument, not improving on it. The one place I departed is recorded below, and a control forced it rather than taste.
think The key is the stamp, the number is a view, and the view is allocated by the anointed remote. That last clause is the whole of it. A number read out of a local tree is allocated per tree, so two stars reading their own trees both read the same next number and both are right -- there is no amount of care that avoids it, which is exactly why six rounds of care did not. The allocator has to live somewhere only one of them is, and that is xy.
think The boundary is what makes it safe rather than merely clever. A row that has reached xy is shared and its number is frozen, because 2,519 citations of %N stand in the tree and 532 sit in commit bodies that can never be edited. So renumbering is confined to unshared rows, and the witness proves the confinement rather than trusting it -- a published number that moves is the one thing the design forbids outright, and it is the gated reading.
think My first draft named two readings, collision and rebound, and the control refused to let me keep them. Case 2 planted a new row squatting a number upstream had spent and the scan reported BOTH counters at 1, because comparing (number, stamp) sets cannot tell a squatter from an edited stamp -- in both, a published number points somewhere else here. Two readings that always fire together are one reading wearing two names, and a control watching the wrong counter would have passed a broken gate. Collapsed to one gate with the diagnosis telling them apart. The control found that, not me.
think Two more of the control's cases were my expectations being wrong rather than the tool -- next_free after a row is already booked is the one AFTER it, twice. Worth writing down: I wrote the assertion from what I intended and the tool answered from what it computed, and the tool was right both times. A control that only ever confirms the author is a control that has not been consulted.
think Then the proof I actually trust. Plants prove the code; they do not prove the code would have caught the thing that happened. The pre-rebase commits still existed, so I replayed the morning: worktree at 30252a24f7, which is this tree holding my %292, against 41a4b0950a, which is the anointed spine already holding the shed lane's %292. It answers squatting %292, names both stamps, and prints next_free=294 -- the exact repair two rebases and a hand-renumber cost, printed before a push would have happened.
think Scope discipline on the dated documents. My first pass rewrote the Status line AND a body paragraph in two dated design files, which is a Tier 2 breach for tidiness. Restored both bodies verbatim and accreted the seat as an addendum instead. A Status field is a living pointer and may move; the argument beneath it is testimony and may not. The three-writers doc still reads proposed, awaiting a seat, with the word recorded underneath it, which is the honest shape.
obs The gate reads zero on a tree whose whole ledger predates the law, which is the point: every existing %N is shared and frozen, scars included, and the law governs allocation from here forward rather than retroactively tidying 297 rows and every citation of them.
obs What this does not close is named on the card. %281 and %291 -- one tree per star, or a lock -- are the build and source-file halves of the same root and stay Keaton's word. This closes the ledger half only, and says so.
